00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:12.430
2018 was a rollercoaster year for Apple.

00:00:12.430 --> 00:00:17.369
In August, its stock hit two-hundred and seven
dollars, making it the first trillion dollar

00:00:17.369 --> 00:00:18.859
company in history.

00:00:18.859 --> 00:00:26.099
Then, in a dramatic few months, it lost $450
billion as Microsoft, Amazon, and Alphabet

00:00:26.099 --> 00:00:27.509
passed it by.

00:00:27.509 --> 00:00:32.140
And for the first time in fifteen years, the
company announced it would make less money

00:00:32.140 --> 00:00:33.380
than expected.

00:00:33.380 --> 00:00:34.450
The problem?

00:00:34.450 --> 00:00:36.830
Fewer people buying iPhones.

00:00:36.830 --> 00:00:43.220
The reasons are many: China’s slowing economy,
sticker shock, market saturation.

00:00:43.220 --> 00:00:48.470
But there’s also another big factor: Apple
has a new competitor.

00:00:48.470 --> 00:00:49.760
Not Samsung.

00:00:49.760 --> 00:00:50.920
Not Google.

00:00:50.920 --> 00:00:52.020
Not Huawei.

00:00:52.020 --> 00:00:53.120
…Apple!

00:00:53.120 --> 00:00:56.710
The iPhone is increasingly competing with
itself.

00:00:56.710 --> 00:01:00.430
One, because we’re all holding on to them
longer.

00:01:00.430 --> 00:01:04.190
But also because it doesn’t cost what you
think it does.

00:01:04.190 --> 00:01:09.290
As the price of an iPhone goes up, something
new is happening to its value, and it’s

00:01:09.290 --> 00:01:12.530
shaking up Apple’s entire business model.

00:01:12.530 --> 00:01:17.799
To understand what’s really going on, we
need to calculate the actual, hidden price

00:01:17.799 --> 00:01:21.390
of the iPhone.

00:01:21.390 --> 00:01:26.159
Generally speaking, there are two ways you
can get rich selling things to people:

00:01:26.159 --> 00:01:32.249
One, you can play the numbers game - Sell
a gazillion small things for a few cents profit

00:01:32.249 --> 00:01:33.249
each.

00:01:33.249 --> 00:01:38.159
Think: Gas stations, grocery stores, and,
on a good day, Amazon.

00:01:38.159 --> 00:01:44.249
Sure, you’ll probably lose money at first,
but sell a few more and now you have the power

00:01:44.249 --> 00:01:45.430
of scale.

00:01:45.430 --> 00:01:47.950
Lower costs, higher margins.

00:01:47.950 --> 00:01:48.950
Look at you!

00:01:48.950 --> 00:01:53.109
Or, you can sell a few really expensive things.

00:01:53.109 --> 00:01:58.310
Ever wondered why your town has like three
mattress stores for every one person?

00:01:58.310 --> 00:02:02.540
They’re always empty and yet somehow still
keep the lights on.

00:02:02.540 --> 00:02:07.409
Well, that’s because a few feet of foam
doesn’t cost thousands, or even hundreds,

00:02:07.409 --> 00:02:08.409
of dollars.

00:02:08.409 --> 00:02:13.720
50, 60, 90 percent of a mattress is pure,
king-sized profit.

00:02:13.720 --> 00:02:17.239
It only takes a few sales a month to stay
in business.

00:02:17.239 --> 00:02:19.750
And, therefore, disruption.

00:02:19.750 --> 00:02:25.510
But a few companies, the really, really successful
ones, manage to do both.

00:02:25.510 --> 00:02:30.540
If you can make hundreds of dollars on each
item, sell it thousands of times an hour,

00:02:30.540 --> 00:02:36.060
and convince those same people to buy again
in 12 months, AND make us want to line up

00:02:36.060 --> 00:02:43.040
with a smile on our face for the privilege
of doing it, well, now you have a $265 billion

00:02:43.040 --> 00:02:44.510
a year business.

00:02:44.510 --> 00:02:50.159
The iPhone is, arguably, the most successful
subscription service in history.

00:02:50.159 --> 00:02:55.489
You can bet Apple will announce a new model,
or three, every September, like clockwork,

00:02:55.489 --> 00:02:59.129
just as surely as you know they’ll name
it something weird.

00:02:59.129 --> 00:03:02.940
The problem is, technology is getting really
good.

00:03:02.940 --> 00:03:07.129
So good that a lot of people are thinking
“Why do I need a new one?”

00:03:07.129 --> 00:03:12.519
Some of us care about Portrait Mode and dual
optical image stabilization, but, for most

00:03:12.519 --> 00:03:18.401
people, checking Twitter and using WeChat
is the same on the Phone XS as the X, and

00:03:18.401 --> 00:03:19.760
8, and 7.

00:03:19.760 --> 00:03:23.319
You’re invited to the blue bubble club regardless.

00:03:23.319 --> 00:03:27.890
According to analysts, the average person
waited three years to upgrade their smartphone

00:03:27.890 --> 00:03:29.569
in twenty eighteen.

00:03:29.569 --> 00:03:31.109
This year: four.

00:03:31.109 --> 00:03:35.720
Of course, phones are a little bit weird in
this respect because so are humans.

00:03:35.720 --> 00:03:39.130
The iPhone isn’t just a tool, it’s a status
symbol.

00:03:39.130 --> 00:03:43.200
According to researchers at the University
of Chicago, the best predictors of wealth

00:03:43.200 --> 00:03:50.670
in 1992 were: owning an automatic dishwasher,
a fireplace, and oooh, a garage door opener.

00:03:50.670 --> 00:03:51.670
Hi-tech!

00:03:51.670 --> 00:03:58.189
Today, it’s owning a passport, an iPhone,
and iPad, which have a 70% chance of correctly

00:03:58.189 --> 00:04:00.770
guessing whether you have a high income.

00:04:00.770 --> 00:04:05.360
Owning the newest model signals you have a
grand of disposable income to spend every

00:04:05.360 --> 00:04:06.360
year.

00:04:06.360 --> 00:04:10.499
Add some AirPods and you won’t even hear
the sound of the money leaving your bank account.

00:04:10.499 --> 00:04:15.719
But that only works if this year’s iPhone
looks different from last, and Apple only

00:04:15.719 --> 00:04:18.720
really changes its design every other year.

00:04:18.720 --> 00:04:20.590
So, here we are.

00:04:20.590 --> 00:04:25.320
People are losing their appetite for frequent
upgrades, but Apple certainly hasn’t lost

00:04:25.320 --> 00:04:27.240
its appetite for money.

00:04:27.240 --> 00:04:30.440
Which means, of course, higher prices!

00:04:30.440 --> 00:04:35.310
Selling half as many phones isn’t such a
problem if they’re all twice as expensive.

00:04:35.310 --> 00:04:43.020
The cheaper iPhone has slowly crept from $649,
to 699, to 749, today.

00:04:43.020 --> 00:04:50.960
The more expensive XS now starts at nine ninety-nine
and goes all the way up to $1,449.

00:04:50.960 --> 00:04:54.460
Of course, that’s only if you’re lucky
enough to live in the U.S.

00:04:54.460 --> 00:04:57.170
And yet, revenue is still down.

00:04:57.170 --> 00:04:58.830
Something’s missing.

00:04:58.830 --> 00:05:03.949
To make sense of this, we need to answer the
question: How expensive is the iPhone, really?

00:05:03.949 --> 00:05:09.150
There’s plenty of back-and-forth on whether
the iPhone is, quote, worth it, but what isn’t

00:05:09.150 --> 00:05:13.430
really debated is that, in general, it’s
higher-priced than most other smartphones

00:05:13.430 --> 00:05:15.520
from most other brands.

00:05:15.520 --> 00:05:20.889
Being selective about which price segments
it sells to is pretty much written in Apple’s

00:05:20.889 --> 00:05:21.889
DNA.

00:05:21.889 --> 00:05:27.000
There’s no $300 MacBook not because it’s
impossible but because that’s just not what

00:05:27.000 --> 00:05:28.000
it’s about.

00:05:28.000 --> 00:05:33.090
And with prices going up even higher, it would
seem that’s never been more true.

00:05:33.090 --> 00:05:36.380
But sticker price isn’t the best way to
measure actual cost.

00:05:36.380 --> 00:05:39.510
In fact, it’s pretty misleading.

00:05:39.510 --> 00:05:41.180
Phones aren’t consumable.

00:05:41.180 --> 00:05:45.340
Unless you pull a Black Mirror, they’re
still worth something in a year or two.

00:05:45.340 --> 00:05:49.970
The price of something like a house accounts
for how much it’s expected to gain or lose

00:05:49.970 --> 00:05:50.970
in value.

00:05:50.970 --> 00:05:56.449
Usually, the structure itself depreciates
- styles change and wood rots, but the land

00:05:56.449 --> 00:05:58.050
underneath it appreciates.

00:05:58.050 --> 00:06:01.750
Which, side note, is why tiny houses aren’t
investments.

00:06:01.750 --> 00:06:06.729
The land they sit on is often leased, not
owned, and therefore, it only loses value.

00:06:06.729 --> 00:06:10.080
Basically, you’ve just bought a house-shaped
RV!

00:06:10.080 --> 00:06:15.080
Anyway, unless your phone was signed by the
ghost of Steve Jobs himself, it’s more like

00:06:15.080 --> 00:06:17.970
a car - its value goes down.

00:06:17.970 --> 00:06:19.110
And fast.

00:06:19.110 --> 00:06:20.920
But not all phones are the same.

00:06:20.920 --> 00:06:25.900
On average, iPhones lose 45% of their value
in the first 12 months.

00:06:25.900 --> 00:06:28.419
Samsung phones, 62%.

00:06:28.419 --> 00:06:30.210
and Google, 81.

00:06:30.210 --> 00:06:35.560
That may sound a little unfair - Apple only
makes premium devices, while Samsung’s average

00:06:35.560 --> 00:06:38.130
might be skewed by a few low-end outliers.

00:06:38.130 --> 00:06:40.910
So, let’s look at specific models.

00:06:40.910 --> 00:06:45.229
A year later, the Samsung S8 lost 58% of its
value.

00:06:45.229 --> 00:06:50.160
The Google Pixel XL, 82, and the OnePlus One
94%.

00:06:50.160 --> 00:06:54.370
Meanwhile, the iPhone 8 still lost only 45.

00:06:54.370 --> 00:06:57.860
That’s a difference of hundreds of dollars.

00:06:57.860 --> 00:07:01.460
And that’s before the record-setting iPhone
X.

00:07:01.460 --> 00:07:07.000
In September 2018, only a week before the
XS was set to be announced, the X was still

00:07:07.000 --> 00:07:10.060
worth $679.

00:07:10.060 --> 00:07:13.300
It lost only 32% of its value.

00:07:13.300 --> 00:07:17.950
Taking this into account, we can calculate
a rough Total Cost of Ownership.

00:07:17.950 --> 00:07:23.550
A thousand dollars upfront, minus 679 for
selling it a year later, gives us a real cost

00:07:23.550 --> 00:07:24.870
of $320.

00:07:24.870 --> 00:07:29.860
That’s $26 a month to always have the latest
and greatest.

00:07:29.860 --> 00:07:32.930
Now, to be fair, sticker price does matter.

00:07:32.930 --> 00:07:39.460
If next year’s iPhone cost $20,000, and
sells 12 months later for $19,900, it would

00:07:39.460 --> 00:07:43.220
be a bit disingenuous to call it a $100 phone.

00:07:43.220 --> 00:07:46.990
But, of course, resale value is still very
important.

00:07:46.990 --> 00:07:52.689
And here’s why: Because used iPhones are
now worth more, more people are selling them.

00:07:52.689 --> 00:07:53.810
Higher supply.

00:07:53.810 --> 00:07:58.610
Meanwhile, more people want the new design
but don’t necessarily want to spend a whole

00:07:58.610 --> 00:07:59.620
month’s rent.

00:07:59.620 --> 00:08:00.790
Higher demand.

00:08:00.790 --> 00:08:04.060
The result: a huge, thriving, secondary market.

00:08:04.060 --> 00:08:07.550
Amazon and eBay are flooded with iPhone X’s.

00:08:07.550 --> 00:08:12.830
Which means the new phone has to compete with
its still-pretty-good last generation.

00:08:12.830 --> 00:08:16.600
In other words, Apple is, increasingly, competing
with itself.

00:08:16.600 --> 00:08:21.680
And the better today’s iPhone, the harder
it’ll be to compete against, used, next

00:08:21.680 --> 00:08:22.680
year.

00:08:22.680 --> 00:08:25.889
Now, if you’re an investor, this all sounds
pretty bad.

00:08:25.889 --> 00:08:27.620
Like, sound-the-alarms bad.

00:08:27.620 --> 00:08:31.770
Remember, the iPhone alone accounts for 60%
its revenue.

00:08:31.770 --> 00:08:34.570
Any other company would fight this tooth and
nail.

00:08:34.570 --> 00:08:36.479
So, what’s Apple doing?

00:08:36.479 --> 00:08:42.019
Not only are they not fighting it, they’re
actually embracing it, making the iPhone last

00:08:42.019 --> 00:08:43.620
even longer.

00:08:43.620 --> 00:08:47.940
Instead of buying a whole new phone because
you can’t get 3 hours on a charge, they’ve

00:08:47.940 --> 00:08:51.399
made it easier than ever to just replace the
battery.

00:08:51.399 --> 00:08:56.640
And while Android phones often receive only
two years of software updates, iOS 12 runs

00:08:56.640 --> 00:09:04.029
on all these devices, going all the way back
to the 2013 iPhone 5s and the first iPad Air.

00:09:04.029 --> 00:09:09.140
Even better, it actually speeds up your phone,
it’s no longer a tradeoff of slow-as-molasses

00:09:09.140 --> 00:09:10.240
for new features.

00:09:10.240 --> 00:09:14.740
Or, let’s be real, pressing “Update”
so it’ll stop bothering you.

00:09:14.740 --> 00:09:20.510
All of this is awesome for you and I, but
isn’t it a terrible business idea?

00:09:20.510 --> 00:09:22.150
Not necessarily.

00:09:22.150 --> 00:09:26.860
Unless you buy from Apple.com, second-hand
phones don’t directly put any money in its

00:09:26.860 --> 00:09:30.970
pockets, but Apple has other ways of making
a profit.

00:09:30.970 --> 00:09:37.290
New or used, you still buy apps, You still
subscribe to Apple Music, Apple Video, iCloud,

00:09:37.290 --> 00:09:38.300
and so on.

00:09:38.300 --> 00:09:43.610
The iPhone is a gateway to things like the
iPad, Apple Watch, and AirPods.

00:09:43.610 --> 00:09:47.360
Even if you buy none of these things, you’re
still useful to Apple.

00:09:47.360 --> 00:09:53.240
Google, for example, pays an estimated $9
billion a year just to be your default search

00:09:53.240 --> 00:09:54.240
engine.

00:09:54.240 --> 00:09:55.240
Nine BILLION.

00:09:55.240 --> 00:09:59.769
That’s the GDP of Haiti for one switch to
be flipped.

00:09:59.769 --> 00:10:04.720
User base means control and control, ultimately,
means money.

00:10:04.720 --> 00:10:08.370
In other words, things like services can make
up for lost sales.

00:10:08.370 --> 00:10:13.060
It’s sort-of the in-app purchase model applied
to the iPhone as a whole.

00:10:13.060 --> 00:10:17.670
In the long term, Apple needs to move its
focus away from the iPhone and towards new

00:10:17.670 --> 00:10:19.630
platforms entirely.

00:10:19.630 --> 00:10:22.610
Augmented reality glasses are the future.

00:10:22.610 --> 00:10:26.350
As we saw in 2018, this transition won’t
be easy.

00:10:26.350 --> 00:10:29.600
But it’s the best, and really only path
forward.

00:10:29.600 --> 00:10:33.010
Because, the truth is, what’s happening
now is normal.

00:10:33.010 --> 00:10:35.279
Everything before was an anomaly.

00:10:35.279 --> 00:10:37.470
A very profitable one.

00:10:37.470 --> 00:10:39.950
An easy one for companies to get used to.

00:10:39.950 --> 00:10:42.070
But, still, an anomaly.

00:10:42.070 --> 00:10:48.170
From 2007 until fairly recently, the stars
were perfectly aligned - technology was moving

00:10:48.170 --> 00:10:52.889
incredibly quickly but still always left enough
to be desired for next year.

00:10:52.889 --> 00:10:55.380
The smartphone was in its growth period.

00:10:55.380 --> 00:11:00.540
But the average people buying a new phone
every year or two just isn’t sustainable.

00:11:00.540 --> 00:11:01.540
Not economically.

00:11:01.540 --> 00:11:03.860
And not environmentally.

00:11:03.860 --> 00:11:10.070
Phones should last years and years, go through
two or even three owners, and then, gracefully,

00:11:10.070 --> 00:11:11.450
be recycled.

00:11:11.450 --> 00:11:16.070
The future is one where a few of us buy the
latest phone, take good care of it, and then

00:11:16.070 --> 00:11:19.350
give it back to Apple, or sell it second-hand.

00:11:19.350 --> 00:11:23.300
The problem is, selling your phone is, kinda
the worst.

00:11:23.300 --> 00:11:24.920
How do you know when to sell?

00:11:24.920 --> 00:11:26.030
And for how much?

00:11:26.030 --> 00:11:28.490
So it ends up just sitting in a drawer somewhere.

00:11:28.490 --> 00:11:33.370
Dave, over on Here’s the Bad Version has
an idea for a solution: An app.

00:11:33.370 --> 00:11:37.529
You’d scan your phone when you buy it, tell
it how often you wanna upgrade, and then it

00:11:37.529 --> 00:11:41.160
sends you a notification when it’s the best
time to sell.

00:11:41.160 --> 00:11:46.880
I’m 100% serious when I say I think this
could be a multi-million dollar startup, someone

00:11:46.880 --> 00:11:48.620
just needs to program it.

00:11:48.620 --> 00:11:52.760
You could design the app to make predictions
about how the value of your phone might change

00:11:52.760 --> 00:11:57.399
in the future, for example, with the machine
learning course on Brilliant, which teaches

00:11:57.399 --> 00:12:01.889
you, step by step, intuitively, with its computer
science and math courses.

